WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.860
Saying that a story has a beginning, a

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:03.540
middle, and an end is a useless piece

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:04.890
of information that does nothing to

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.510
describe a story, and more importantly,

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.164
it does nothing to help you tell a story.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.120
When people tell you that stories

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.410
have beginnings, middles, and

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:15.180
endings, it is some of the worst

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:16.800
possible advice they can give you.

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:18.120
If you're hoping to tell better

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.770
stories, let me tell you why.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.390
The reason why this is terrible advice

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.340
is because everything in the world

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:27.990
has a beginning, middle, and end.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:29.275
This morning I got dressed.

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:31.990
It had a beginning, a middle, and an end.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:33.190
I started with my underwear.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:34.120
That was the beginning.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:35.710
Then I put on a shirt and a pair

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:36.940
of pants that was the middle,

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:38.440
and then shoes and socks.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:39.490
That was the end.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:41.350
Does it help people get dressed to know

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:42.970
it has a beginning, a middle, and an end.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.100
No, it doesn't help in any way whatsoever.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:46.600
Everything has a beginning,

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:47.440
a middle, and an end.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:50.170
A Snickers bar has a beginning,

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:51.400
a middle, and an end.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:52.600
It has a beginning.

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:53.620
I open it up.

00:00:54.635 --> 00:00:55.175
I take a bite.

00:00:56.709 --> 00:00:57.694
Oh, it is okay.

00:00:58.025 --> 00:00:58.504
You're not the best

00:00:58.504 --> 00:00:59.555
Snickers bar in the world.

00:00:59.915 --> 00:01:01.294
Stories should have great beginnings.

00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:02.375
Better than this one, by the way.

00:01:02.405 --> 00:01:04.354
Eventually I would get to the middle.

00:01:05.945 --> 00:01:07.145
The middle is no good either.

00:01:07.235 --> 00:01:08.345
And then eventually I

00:01:08.345 --> 00:01:09.274
would get to the end.

00:01:10.384 --> 00:01:10.895
The end.

00:01:11.465 --> 00:01:13.175
Oh, a really bad snookers bar.

00:01:13.324 --> 00:01:13.925
Most are good.

00:01:13.925 --> 00:01:15.425
I think it's 'cause it's so cold.

00:01:15.785 --> 00:01:16.289
It's probably the gas.

00:01:16.635 --> 00:01:18.315
Station that doesn't help you make a

00:01:18.315 --> 00:01:19.725
Snickers bar, doesn't it help you even

00:01:19.725 --> 00:01:21.165
understand what a Snickers bar is?

00:01:21.225 --> 00:01:22.755
Everything has a beginning,

00:01:22.785 --> 00:01:23.655
a middle, and an end.

00:01:23.715 --> 00:01:25.305
My life, as much as I don't

00:01:25.305 --> 00:01:26.475
want to admit to, it has a

00:01:26.475 --> 00:01:27.825
beginning, a middle, and an end.

00:01:27.915 --> 00:01:29.775
I was born, I don't remember it, but

00:01:29.775 --> 00:01:31.365
I was born and that was the beginning.

00:01:31.455 --> 00:01:32.625
I'm now somewhere in the

00:01:32.625 --> 00:01:34.455
middle, and then theoretically,

00:01:34.455 --> 00:01:35.925
hopefully not, but possibly.

00:01:35.925 --> 00:01:37.905
So someday there will be an end.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:39.690
That doesn't define a life

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:40.560
that doesn't even help you

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:41.940
understand how to live a life.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:43.920
Saying that a story has a beginning, a

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.630
middle, and an end is a useless piece

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:46.980
of information that does nothing to

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:48.960
describe a story, and more importantly,

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.760
it does nothing to help you tell a story,

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:53.580
a story.

00:01:53.985 --> 00:01:56.085
Is not a beginning, a middle, and an end.

00:01:56.175 --> 00:01:59.145
A story is a singular moment in your

00:01:59.145 --> 00:02:01.335
life in which something changed.

00:02:01.605 --> 00:02:02.414
You thought about the

00:02:02.414 --> 00:02:03.525
world in a different way.

00:02:03.705 --> 00:02:04.785
You thought about yourself

00:02:04.785 --> 00:02:05.595
in a different way.

00:02:05.745 --> 00:02:07.455
You changed fundamentally

00:02:07.455 --> 00:02:08.264
as a human being.

00:02:08.625 --> 00:02:11.175
A singular moment in time that is

00:02:11.175 --> 00:02:13.365
wrapped around the context that

00:02:13.365 --> 00:02:14.954
brings that singular moment to

00:02:14.954 --> 00:02:16.365
the greatest clarity possible.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:19.380
One moment in time, what I

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:20.670
call a five second moment.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:21.450
'cause these changes

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:22.800
happen instantaneously.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:24.930
A five second moment with the context

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:26.850
dropped around it to make that five

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.340
second moment as clear as possible to

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:30.690
the audience that you're speaking to.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:33.300
That is what a story is, a singular moment

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.280
in time where something changed about

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:36.835
you or your perception of the world.

00:02:37.365 --> 00:02:39.825
And the context that you wrap around

00:02:39.825 --> 00:02:42.225
that moment to bring it to the greatest

00:02:42.255 --> 00:02:44.115
clarity possible to your audience.

00:02:44.115 --> 00:02:45.825
And that is every single movie you've

00:02:45.825 --> 00:02:47.655
ever seen, every TV show you've ever

00:02:47.655 --> 00:02:49.365
watched, every book you've ever read, any

00:02:49.365 --> 00:02:51.255
play or musical you've seen on the stage,

00:02:51.285 --> 00:02:53.745
and any story that I have ever told.

00:02:53.835 --> 00:02:55.245
If it doesn't have a five second

00:02:55.275 --> 00:02:57.375
moment of transformation, it's not bad.

00:02:57.435 --> 00:02:59.204
It's just called an anecdote.

00:02:59.565 --> 00:03:00.825
It's just a moment in

00:03:00.825 --> 00:03:02.535
time that is amusing and.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:05.240
Silly and delightful, but doesn't have

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:07.310
the stickiness, the unforgettable ness,

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:09.260
the staying power, the connectiveness,

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:11.420
the relatability of an actual story.

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:12.470
They're not bad.

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:13.490
They just have to understand.

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:14.030
They don't have the

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:15.680
power of an actual story.

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:17.360
So when you're thinking about

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:18.500
storytelling and you're thinking

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:20.090
about, I'm gonna craft a story.

00:03:20.135 --> 00:03:21.665
I'm gonna produce a great story

00:03:21.665 --> 00:03:23.045
for the screen or the stage,

00:03:23.225 --> 00:03:25.114
or maybe just on my first date.

00:03:25.265 --> 00:03:26.524
The last thing you should think

00:03:26.524 --> 00:03:28.054
about, the very, very last thing

00:03:28.054 --> 00:03:29.765
you should think about is it needs

00:03:29.765 --> 00:03:31.715
a beginning, a middle, and an end.

00:03:31.924 --> 00:03:33.845
Because no matter what you say, no

00:03:33.845 --> 00:03:35.225
matter what the length of the thing

00:03:35.225 --> 00:03:37.625
you say is, it will always inherently

00:03:37.625 --> 00:03:39.334
have a beginning, a middle, and an end.

00:03:39.394 --> 00:03:40.864
But does that help you in any way?

00:03:40.864 --> 00:03:41.644
It doesn't.

00:03:41.704 --> 00:03:43.325
Everything has a beginning, a

00:03:43.325 --> 00:03:44.855
middle, and an end, so it is

00:03:44.855 --> 00:03:47.105
stupid, meaningless advice.

00:03:47.145 --> 00:03:48.885
For crafting better stories.

00:03:48.945 --> 00:03:50.985
Instead, find a moment.

00:03:51.495 --> 00:03:53.115
Find a moment that means

00:03:53.115 --> 00:03:54.705
something to you in this world.

00:03:55.005 --> 00:03:57.195
Wrap the context around that moment

00:03:57.195 --> 00:03:59.415
to bring clarity so that the audience

00:03:59.415 --> 00:04:01.245
understands what you're saying and then

00:04:01.245 --> 00:04:03.285
deliver it in the best possible way.

00:04:03.465 --> 00:04:05.385
That is the way to tell a great story.

